  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with a custom GLSL water surface under a gradient sky dome. It passes typecheck. I couldn't run `vite build` or load the page (no shell or browser), so I never saw a rendered frame. Shader compile errors, the colour balance and the first-frame flag are all unchecked.\n\n- **Water mesh:** a 321×321 grid, denser near the camera and sparser toward the horizon, so the waves are real geometry. The vertex shader adds four travelling sine waves driven by the engine's `uTime`.\n- **Water shading:**\n  - A fresnel term mixes in more sky reflection at grazing angles.\n  - A noise-based ripple perturbs the normals, fading with distance.\n  - A sun specular glint comes from a Blinn-Phong lobe on the rippled normal.\n  - Far water blends into the sky's horizon colour.\n- **Sky:** a large sphere around the camera with a horizon-to-zenith gradient and a sun glow and disc. The water reflects the same sky function, so the reflection matches the dome.\n- **Camera:** it sits 3 units above the water and looks toward the horizon.\n- **Colour:** shaders output linear light and the engine's default tone mapping and sRGB encoding handle the rest. The sky and water colours are tuned by estimate, so check they aren't washed out or too dark.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set from a `requestAnimationFrame` queued in the first `onFrame` callback. It is the likeliest thing to need adjusting if the harness never sees it.",
